<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deodoro da Fonseca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as General Deodoro da Fonseca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deodoro da Fonseca was a Brazilian army officer who led the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-republic-brazil">coup of 15 November 1889</a> that overthrew Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>. He proclaimed the republic and became the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deodoro-da-fonseca.jpg" alt="Deodoro da Fonseca" width="282" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deodoro_da_Fonseca_(1889).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Marshal <b>Deodoro da Fonseca</b> led the coup of November 1889 with little planning and even less public mobilisation, Pedro II offered no resistance, and the army that had won the Paraguayan War quietly ended the empire that had sent it to fight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 1810s and 1820s, and presidential republics became the rule in the Americas: Mexico’s federal constitution of 1824, Argentina’s of 1853, closely modelled on that of the United States, and Brazil’s after the fall of its monarchy in 1889, when Marshal <b>Deodoro da Fonseca</b> became its first president.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Protests followed, and <b>Deodoro da Fonseca</b>, head of the Provisional Government, who was said to prefer the old music himself, kept da Silva's composition as the national anthem by a decree of 20 January 1890; the winning piece became the Anthem of the Proclamation of the Republic.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>Marshal <b>Deodoro da Fonseca</b> led a coup in Rio de Janeiro that deposed Emperor Pedro II and proclaimed the Republic of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 15 November 1889 army officers led by Marshal <b>Deodoro da Fonseca</b> overthrew Emperor Pedro II, a year after the abolition of slavery had cost the monarchy the support of planters.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marechal Deodoro, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in Barra Mansa, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-9.71,-35.9&amp;year=1869" aria-label="Marechal Deodoro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3560 2526 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Deodoro da Fonseca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4010 2796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4010 2796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-9.71,-35.9&amp;year=1869">What was here in 1869? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
